\title{normet: Meteorological Normalisation, Decomposition, and Counterfactual Modelling for Air Quality Time Series}
\description{
\if{html}{\figure{logo.png}{options: style='float: right' alt='logo' width='120'}}

Provides a suite of tools for advanced air quality time series analysis. Core functionalities include meteorological normalisation, which normalises away the weather-driven variation to reveal underlying trends in data like air pollution concentrations, and causal inference using both classic and machine learning-based Synthetic Control Methods. The package features high-level workflows for uncertainty quantification, time-series decomposition, and robust placebo tests, powered by automated machine learning in H2O or by gradient-boosting back ends.
}
\details{
\code{normet} brings three layers together behind one API:
\enumerate{
\item \strong{Weather normalisation.} \code{\link[=nm_do_all]{nm_do_all()}} and \code{\link[=nm_normalise]{nm_normalise()}} train a
LightGBM or H2O model of the pollutant on weather and time, then
resample the meteorology many times to remove its contribution and
reveal the emission-driven signal.
\item \strong{Decomposition and uncertainty.} \code{\link[=nm_decompose]{nm_decompose()}} splits a series
into its emission- and meteorology-driven parts;
\code{\link[=nm_decompose_uncertainty]{nm_decompose_uncertainty()}} attributes the spread of a de-weathered
estimate to weather, model form and their interaction;
\code{\link[=nm_rolling]{nm_rolling()}} and \code{\link[=nm_trend]{nm_trend()}} follow the emission-driven signal over
time.
\item \strong{Causal inference.} \code{\link[=nm_run_scm]{nm_run_scm()}} and \code{\link[=nm_run_scm_staggered]{nm_run_scm_staggered()}} build
synthetic controls from untreated donor units, with placebo tests and
uncertainty bands.
}

